readings.c1s1:
§ 12.

¶
'''uneven''' (un-ē′vn), ''a.'' [< ME. ''uneven'', < AS. ''unefen'', < ''un-'', not, + ''efen'', even: see ''un-''<sup>1</sup> and ''even''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' Not even. {{smaller|(''a'') Not level, smooth, or plain; rough; rugged. ''Shak.'', M. N. D., iii. 2. 417. (''b'') Not straight or direct; crooked. ''Shak.'', R. and J., iv. 1. 5. (''c'') Not uniform, equable, regular, or continuous; changeable; jerky.}}
{{smaller block|Light quirks of music, broken and ''uneven''.
{{right|''Pope'', To the Earl of Burlington, Ep. 4.}}}}
{{smaller|(''d'') Not perfectly horizontal or level, as the beam of a scale; not at the same height or on the same plane; hence, not fair, just, or true.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Belial, in much ''uneven'' scale thou weigh’st
All others by thyself.</poem>
{{right|''Milton'', P. R., ii. 173.}}}}
{{smaller|(''e'') In ''arith.'', odd; not divisible by 2 without a remainder: as, 3, 5, 7, etc., are ''uneven'' numbers.}} '''2†.''' Ill-matched; unsuitable; ill-assorted. ''Spenser'', F. Q., VI. v. 9.—'''3†.''' Difficult; perplexing; embarrassing. ''Shak.'', 1 Hen. IV., i. 1. 50.—'''Uneven pages''', {{smaller|pages with odd numbers, like 1, 3, 5, 7, etc.}}

'''unevenly''' (un-ē′vn-li), ''adv.'' [< ME. ''unevenly'', < ''uneven'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] In an uneven manner; not smoothly or regularly.—'''Unevenly even.''' {{smaller|See ''even''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''unevenness''' (un-ē′vn-nes), ''n.'' The state or character of being uneven. {{smaller|(''a'') Inequality of surface: as, the ''unevenness'' of ground or of roads. (''b'') Irregularity; want of uniformity. (''c'') Want of equableness; unsteadiness; variableness.}}
{{smaller block|''Unevenness'' of temper. {{right|''Addison'', Spectator.}}
Her abruptness and ''unevenness'' of manner were plainly the result of her secluded and lowly circumstances.
{{right|''George Eliot'', Mill on the Floss, vi. 6.}}}}
{{smaller|(''d'') Want of smoothness in regard to style or composition. ''Boyle'', Works, II. 251.}}

'''uneventful''' (un-ē-vent′fu̇l), ''a.'' Not eventful.

readings.c3s2:
¶
'''unfailingly''' (un-fā′ling-li), ''adv.'' In an unfailing manner; surely.

'''unfailingness''' (un-fā′ling-nes), ''n.'' The character of being unfailing. ''Bp. Hall'', Sermon on 2 Pet. i. 10.

'''unfain†''' (un-fān′), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unfain'', ''unfein'', ''unfawe'', < AS. ''unfægen'', < ''un-'', not, + ''fægen'', glad: see ''fain''<sup>1</sup>.] Not fain; sorry.
{{smaller block|All the folke were ''vnfayn'', & of fyn will<br>To haue reft hir the rynke, for ruth that thai had.
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 12108.}}
“A-las,” sche sayd, “I am ''vn-fayn''<br>To se my sone in this dissesse.”
{{right|''Political Poems'', etc. (ed. Furnivall), p. 210.}}}}

'''unfainly†''', ''adv.'' [< ''unfain'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] Sorrowfully. ''Halliwell.''

'''unfainting''' (un-fān′ting), ''a.'' Not fainting; not sinking or succumbing or giving way.
{{smaller block|Thorow which [labyrinth it is] impossible to passe without the conduct of wisdome and exercise of ''unfainting'' fortitude. {{right|''Sandys'', Travailes, p. 88.}}}}

'''unfair''' (un-fãr′), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unfair'', < AS. ''unfæger'' (= Goth. ''unfagrs''), < ''un-'', not, + ''fæger'', beautiful: see ''fair''<sup>1</sup>.] Not fair. {{smaller|(''a'') Not beautiful; not comely. (''b''†) Not glad; sad; sorrowful.}}
{{smaller block|Noght sesyng of sorow, & sobbyng ''vnfaire''<br>On dayes to Endure, with drouping on nightes.
{{right|''Destruction of Troy'' (E. E. T. S.), l. 3290.}}}}
{{smaller|(''c''†) Unseemly; disgraceful.}}
{{smaller block|He watȝ corsed for his vnclannes, & cached ther-inne,<br>Done doun of his dyngneté for dedeȝ ''vnfayre''.
{{right|''Alliterative Poems'' (ed. Morris), ii. 1801.}}}}
{{smaller|(''d'') Not honest; not impartial; disingenuous; using trick or artifice.}}

readings.c3s3:
¶
{{smaller block|You come, like an ''unfair'' merchant, to charge me with being in your debt.
{{right|''Swift.''}}}}
{{smaller block|(''e'') Not based on honesty, justice, or fairness; inequitable: as, ''unfair'' advantages; ''unfair'' practices. ='''Syn.''' (''d'') (''e'') Unjust, inequitable, partial, one-sided, dishonest, dishonorable. See ''candid.''}}

'''unfair''' (un-fãr′), ''v. t.'' To deprive of fairness or beauty. ''Shak.'', Sonnets, v. [Rare.]

'''unfairly''' (un-fãr′li), ''adv.'' In an unfair or unjust manner. ''Secker'', Sermons, IV. xiii.

'''unfairness''' (un-fãr′nes), ''n.'' The state or character of being unfair, in any sense. ''Bentley'', Phileleutherus Lipsiensis.

'''unfaith''' (un-fāth′), ''n.'' Want or absence of faith; distrust.
{{smaller block|''Unfaith'' in aught is want of faith in all.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Merlin and Vivien (song).}}}}

'''unfaithful''' (un-fāth′fu̇l), ''a.'' [< ME. ''unfaythfull''; < ''un-''<sup>1</sup> + ''faithful.''] '''1.''' Not faithful; not observant of promises, vows, allegiance, or duty.
{{smaller block|<poem>Fro all fandyng ''vnfaythfull'' thou fende vs,
Here in this worlde of liffe whille we laste.</poem>
{{right|''York Plays'', p. 241.}}
<poem>His honor rooted in dishonor stood,
And faith ''unfaithful'' kept him falsely true.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Lancelot and Elaine.}}}}
'''2.''' Not performing the proper duty or function.
{{smaller block|My feet through wine ''unfaithful'' to their weight.
{{right|''Pope.''}}}}
